{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TMUUSFZT96ySWAJsSNqk2f","type":"tool_result","content":"go: downloading modernc.org/sqlite v1.62.0\ngo: downloading github.com/golang-jwt/jwt/v4 v4.5.2\ngo: downloading github.com/google/go-querystring v1.2.0\ngo: downloading golang.org/x/sync v0.24.0\ngo: downloading golang.org/x/mod v0.42.0\ngo: downloading golang.org/x/sys v0.49.0\ngo: downloading modernc.org/libc v1.80.1\ngo: downloading github.com/google/uuid v1.6.0\ngo: downloading github.com/ncruces/go-strftime v1.1.0\ngo: downloading github.com/mattn/go-isatty v0.0.24\ngo: downloading modernc.org/mathutil v1.7.1\ngo: downloading modernc.org/memory v1.12.1\ngo: downloading github.com/dustin/go-humanize v1.1.0\ngo: downloading github.com/remyoudompheng/bigfft v0.0.0-20230129092748-24d4a6f8daec\ninternal/roles/testdata/docs/skips/src/_x.go:3:8: expected ')', found 'EOF'\nok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/wtlock\t2.859s\nok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/gitx\t74.638s\nok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/committer\t51.836s\n132:**Fetched inputs (#300).** A worker that fetches bytes from the network records each one under `fetched:` in its role file. Close-out parses the list strictly, like `findings`: an entry with a non-https URL (or one with userinfo, whitespace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\n134:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. For now the outcome ends like `needs-human`: nothing is pushed, no PR is opened, and the comment lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The bounce that fetches the inputs and re-runs the worker comes in a later part.\n181:**One committer for main.** (Concurrency rule, #411: the clone's lock is exclusive for worktree add/remove/prune and shared for fetches, so a lane's fetch never reads a half-added or half-removed worktree; commits and pushes take neither.) Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init --pr`'s (local mode writes only the working tree, #303): it commits only `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/ard-init.lock` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n185:**Directories outlive their issues as the audit trail.** The janitor loop (`internal/janitor`) wakes daily but works once per 30 days (the timestamp is in memory, so a restart costs one extra harmless pass). It lists `.ard/` on a fresh fetch of `main`, finds closed issues (not PRs) with a directory whose close date is more than 90 days old, re-fetches each to catch a reopen, skips any carrying `ard:keep` or `ard:hold`, and deletes the issue's files through the main committer as `ard(janitor:prune)`, in requests of at most 256 files. Only numbered issue directories are candidates, and the committer's path guard enforces it; git history keeps what was deleted. Pause stops it.\n279:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role's `diff_max_lines`, 3,000 changed lines by default). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present. When the change needs files from the network it adds a `### Network inputs` subsection (exact URL, pinned version, verification source, landing path, and who provides it: the worker if its role has network, else a human); it never fetches and never writes a checksum it did not read from a file (#301).\n306:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, exclusively, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands and a fetch aborts if it reads a worktree entry that is half added or removed (#411); fetches take the same lock shared (they still run in parallel), and commits and pushes take neither. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Under `executor: k8s` the `pre` container instead gets a per-job installation token scoped to contents:read on the job's repositories (`github.AppAuth.MintReadToken`), revoked when the job ends; a personal access token cannot be scoped down, so that executor needs GitHub App auth. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. **Default agent image under the local executor (#244, part of #55).** `ard serve` and `ard config check` read the host setting `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`: a digest-pinned reference (`<name>@sha256:<64 hex>`) or a local image ID (`sha256:<hex>` from `make agent-image`); tags are refused (`executor.CheckImageRef`, `ErrImageRef`), and an invalid value stops startup. When it is set, `Prepare` runs every job that reads untrusted input and has no `Dockerfile` (triage, worker agent, test and fix steps, reviewer, and LLM scanner jobs) under Docker in that image, with the same flags and mounts as a Dockerfile role (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, worktree at `/work`, role directory `:ro`, and so on). A role's own `Dockerfile` wins. Deterministic scanners (trusted input) stay on the host. A local image is used as is; a digest that is not present is pulled. There is no fallback to host mode: `ard serve` runs `docker version` at startup and fails if Docker is down, and a job whose Docker setup fails at `Prepare` fails there. The controller's clone is not mounted, so `git` has no repository inside the container; every Docker job gets `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false` so `go build` does not fail on VCS stamping. Unset keeps today's behaviour. With it set, `ard config check` and `ard serve` no longer log or record `executor.host_untrusted` for roles it covers, and the hint of the warning names `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n314:| Workspace | `git worktree` of the controller's clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`) under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/<job-id>`; sidecar exported into the job home | a bare Pod whose `pre` init container fetches the repo at `BaseSHA` with a per-job read-only installation token and runs the same `workspace` code as local `Prepare` into size-limited `emptyDir` volumes; the agent gets `work` with no `.git` |\n321:Kubernetes-executor details (decided, design revision 3 of [#54](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/54), recorded in [#312](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/312); the work is split across later parts of #54, and #317 splits into the API layer and the executor). There is no job API on `ard serve`. **Pod shape.** `pre` fetches with the token passed through `http.extraheader` from the environment, never argv or `.git/config`; the git dir is on a `ctl` volume the agent does not mount; the role dir comes from `cfg.SHA`, so the k8s executor refuses a config without a SHA; sidecars are at pinned SHAs; the untrusted inputs and the scrub list travel in the per-job Secret, capped at 768 KiB (over the cap is a permanent failure with a named cause); `pre` copies the static `ard` binary. The `agent` container mounts only `work` and `out` and holds only its model credential via `secretKeyRef`: no GitHub token, no key, no Secret volume, no SA token. It tees stdout to `out/stdout`, enforces its own deadline, writes a done marker and reaps orphans as PID 1. `post` has its own pinned image and never runs a binary from a shared volume. It serves one route, `GET /v1/result`, on the pod IP, guarded by a preshared 32-byte `crypto/rand` key unique to the pod, mounted only in `post` and kept in memory by the controller, compared in constant time. It answers 409 until the agent has terminated and the marker exists, builds the tar in Go and never runs git, serves one request at a time, has header and idle timeouts, never logs Authorization, and exits 0 after the controller confirms or at the deadline. The transport is plain HTTP inside the cluster (the owner's choice): the key grants only this job's result, and a NetworkPolicy limits who can connect.\n362:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, never invents or recalls a value, records each file in `fetched:` and in a `CAPTURE.md` beside the fixtures, and commits a regen script and Make target; with `Network: unavailable.` it answers `needs-human` listing the inputs it needs (#301).\n373:2. **Pin the head SHA.** Before claiming, the loop fetches the branch into the controller's clone and requires the tip to equal the PR's head SHA; on a mismatch it retries on the next poll. The match also shows the head is this repository's branch, so a fork PR with a colliding branch name is never reviewed. The job's worktree is cut at that commit, so the review describes exactly one commit even if the branch moves.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"38976e44-80ce-455e-8dbe-f5e72b5cfc9d","uuid":"4e5782f4-4a2f-46e6-8836-f5bbc37e3685","timestamp":"2026-11-25T14:20:27.656Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"go: downloading modernc.org/sqlite v1.62.0\ngo: downloading github.com/golang-jwt/jwt/v4 v4.5.2\ngo: downloading github.com/google/go-querystring v1.2.0\ngo: downloading golang.org/x/sync v0.24.0\ngo: downloading golang.org/x/mod v0.42.0\ngo: downloading golang.org/x/sys v0.49.0\ngo: downloading modernc.org/libc v1.80.1\ngo: downloading github.com/google/uuid v1.6.0\ngo: downloading github.com/ncruces/go-strftime v1.1.0\ngo: downloading github.com/mattn/go-isatty v0.0.24\ngo: downloading modernc.org/mathutil v1.7.1\ngo: downloading modernc.org/memory v1.12.1\ngo: downloading github.com/dustin/go-humanize v1.1.0\ngo: downloading github.com/remyoudompheng/bigfft v0.0.0-20230129092748-24d4a6f8daec\ninternal/roles/testdata/docs/skips/src/_x.go:3:8: expected ')', found 'EOF'\nok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/wtlock\t2.859s\nok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/gitx\t74.638s\nok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/committer\t51.836s\n132:**Fetched inputs (#300).** A worker that fetches bytes from the network records each one under `fetched:` in its role file. Close-out parses the list strictly, like `findings`: an entry with a non-https URL (or one with userinfo, whitespace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\n134:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. For now the outcome ends like `needs-human`: nothing is pushed, no PR is opened, and the comment lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The bounce that fetches the inputs and re-runs the worker comes in a later part.\n181:**One committer for main.** (Concurrency rule, #411: the clone's lock is exclusive for worktree add/remove/prune and shared for fetches, so a lane's fetch never reads a half-added or half-removed worktree; commits and pushes take neither.) Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init --pr`'s (local mode writes only the working tree, #303): it commits only `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/ard-init.lock` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n185:**Directories outlive their issues as the audit trail.** The janitor loop (`internal/janitor`) wakes daily but works once per 30 days (the timestamp is in memory, so a restart costs one extra harmless pass). It lists `.ard/` on a fresh fetch of `main`, finds closed issues (not PRs) with a directory whose close date is more than 90 days old, re-fetches each to catch a reopen, skips any carrying `ard:keep` or `ard:hold`, and deletes the issue's files through the main committer as `ard(janitor:prune)`, in requests of at most 256 files. Only numbered issue directories are candidates, and the committer's path guard enforces it; git history keeps what was deleted. Pause stops it.\n279:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role's `diff_max_lines`, 3,000 changed lines by default). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present. When the change needs files from the network it adds a `### Network inputs` subsection (exact URL, pinned version, verification source, landing path, and who provides it: the worker if its role has network, else a human); it never fetches and never writes a checksum it did not read from a file (#301).\n306:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, exclusively, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands and a fetch aborts if it reads a worktree entry that is half added or removed (#411); fetches take the same lock shared (they still run in parallel), and commits and pushes take neither. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Under `executor: k8s` the `pre` container instead gets a per-job installation token scoped to contents:read on the job's repositories (`github.AppAuth.MintReadToken`), revoked when the job ends; a personal access token cannot be scoped down, so that executor needs GitHub App auth. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. **Default agent image under the local executor (#244, part of #55).** `ard serve` and `ard config check` read the host setting `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`: a digest-pinned reference (`<name>@sha256:<64 hex>`) or a local image ID (`sha256:<hex>` from `make agent-image`); tags are refused (`executor.CheckImageRef`, `ErrImageRef`), and an invalid value stops startup. When it is set, `Prepare` runs every job that reads untrusted input and has no `Dockerfile` (triage, worker agent, test and fix steps, reviewer, and LLM scanner jobs) under Docker in that image, with the same flags and mounts as a Dockerfile role (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, worktree at `/work`, role directory `:ro`, and so on). A role's own `Dockerfile` wins. Deterministic scanners (trusted input) stay on the host. A local image is used as is; a digest that is not present is pulled. There is no fallback to host mode: `ard serve` runs `docker version` at startup and fails if Docker is down, and a job whose Docker setup fails at `Prepare` fails there. The controller's clone is not mounted, so `git` has no repository inside the container; every Docker job gets `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false` so `go build` does not fail on VCS stamping. Unset keeps today's behaviour. With it set, `ard config check` and `ard serve` no longer log or record `executor.host_untrusted` for roles it covers, and the hint of the warning names `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor i